<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shatt al-Arab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arvand Rud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shatt al-Arab is the river formed by the joining of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Its lower course, where the Karun joins it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a>, forms part of the border between Iraq and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its marshes and canals sheltered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> rebels in the 9th century, and its control was a cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab.jpg" alt="Shatt al-Arab" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aziz1005 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shat_al-arab-22.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
